    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698230/research-officer": {
      "title": "RESEARCH OFFICER",
      "description": "RESEARCH OFFICER Job No.: 698230 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Fixed-term (Part-time), fraction (0.4) Duration: 3 years fixed-term appointment Remuneration: HEW 5 ($86,344 - $99,179) plus 17% superannuation Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity The School of Philosophical, Historical and Indigenous Studies (SoPHIS) within the Faculty of Arts is seeking a Research Officer to support the ARC Discovery project Australia’s first history of disability: Making a more inclusive Australia . Working under the direction of Dr Kate Murphy (Lead Chief Investigator), this part-time (0.4 FTE) position provides research services and administrative coordination to ensure the delivery of project outcomes. Key responsibilities include: Conducting bibliographical and archival research relevant to the project objectives. Providing administrative support for the processing, handling, and preservation of oral history interviews. Assisting with data collection, entry, preliminary analysis, and literature reviews. Maintaining project documentation, scheduling bookings, and supporting compliance with research ethics and data management protocols. Maintaining effective communication with team members, research partners, and external stakeholders. About You You will bring a methodical approach to research administration alongside strong organisational and analytical skills. Specifically, you will have: A tertiary qualification in a relevant field, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training. Demonstrated project administration skills within a research context, with the ability to manage timelines and meet deadlines. Proven capability in data analysis, record-keeping, and maintaining high standards of detail, accuracy, and confidentiality. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with experience drafting administrative and research documentation. High-level computer literacy and adaptiveness in learning new software tools. The capacity to work effectively both independently and as part of a collaborative team. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something groundbreaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Please submit a cover letter outlining why you are interested in this position and what makes you a strong fit for the role. Please also highlight any relevant research experience, including archival, historical or oral history research, and explain how your experience could contribute to the aims of the Discovery Project. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Dr Kate Murphy, Kate.Murphy@Monash.edu , +61 3 9905 1184 Position Description: Research Officer Applications Close: Monday 26 October, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/697890/research-fellow-in-cardiovascular-stem-cell-research": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Cardiovascular Stem Cell Research",
      "description": "Research Fellow in Cardiovascular Stem Cell Research Job No.: 697890 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 3-year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact as a Research Fellow at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative research community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people advancing them The Opportunity Join the Grant Group to investigate the molecular and functional mechanisms underlying congenital heart defects through multidisciplinary research. This position will use human stem cell-derived cardiomyocyte and cardiac organoid models to investigate cardiac development and function, combining experimental cardiac electrophysiology with genomics and bioinformatics approaches. The role will contribute to the design and delivery of defined research projects, with opportunities to develop new experimental and analytical approaches within a collaborative research environment. The position will involve conducting research with limited supervision, contributing to collaborative projects and disseminating findings through peer-reviewed publications, preprints, conference presentations and seminars. Research activities will include advanced laboratory techniques, cardiac electrophysiology, genomics data analysis and experimental design, alongside mentoring undergraduate and postgraduate students and providing research-related advice. The role will also contribute to the broader activities of the research group, including meetings, limited teaching and the development of research-related materials. We are seeking someone suited to research in a relevant discipline supported by a master’s degree and/or progress towards a doctorate, or a doctoral qualification in a relevant or closely related field. Demonstrated expertise in human stem cell models, cardiac electrophysiology such as patch-clamp recordings, and bioinformatics analysis of genomics datasets such as scRNA-seq will be important, together with strong analytical, manuscript preparation, communication, planning and organisational skills. Join the Grant Group and contribute to research advancing understanding of congenital heart defects — we look forward to receiving your application. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Dianne Ruka, Institute Manager, Australian Regenerative Medicine Institute, Dianne.Ruka@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow Applications Close: Tuesday 3 November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698114/international-recruitment-campaigns-manager": {
      "title": "International Recruitment Campaigns Manager",
      "description": "International Recruitment Campaigns Manager Job No.: 698114 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full time Duration: Continuing appointment Remuneration: $124,343 - $137,251 pa HEW Level 08 (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity International student recruitment is competitive, complex, and constantly shifting. This continuing, full-time role based in Mulgrave will suit an experienced recruitment campaign and engagement professional who thrives on translating market insight into campaigns that move prospective students from curiosity to enrolment. In this important role, you will lead the strategy and execution of international recruitment campaigns across key source markets. Your core responsibilities will include: Developing and managing end-to-end international recruitment campaigns aligned to enrolment targets Analysing market data and campaign performance to inform ongoing optimisation Collaborating with regional teams, agents, and internal stakeholders to ensure consistent, accurate messaging Overseeing campaign budgets and reporting outcomes to senior leadership What You'll Bring Demonstrated experience managing international student recruitment campaigns Strong understanding of key source markets including South-East Asia, South Asia, or China Demonstrated fluency in Mandarin and experience in deploying recruitment campaigns in non English speaking countries Proven ability to translate enrolment strategy into targeted, multi-channel campaign activity Experience working within a university or large education provider environment Confident stakeholder management skills across cross-functional and geographically dispersed teams Proficiency in campaign analytics tools and CRM-driven marketing approaches This is a role for someone who brings both strategic clarity and hands-on execution - someone who understands the complexities of international higher education and can translate insights into effective student recruitment and engagement strategies that respond to the needs of prospective students throughout their decision-making process. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something groundbreaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Ms Karen Zhang, Associate Director Established Markets +61 409 182 034 Position Description: International Recruitment Campaigns Manager Applications Close: Tuesday 20 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
