    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698479/research-fellow-implementation-and-evaluation-translational-medicine": {
      "title": "Research Fellow (Implementation and Evaluation), Translational Medicine",
      "description": "Research Fellow (Implementation and Evaluation), Translational Medicine Job No.: 698479 Location: The Alfred Centre Employment Type: Part-time, fraction (0.4) Duration: 12-month fixed-term appointment Remuneration: Pro-rata of $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) or Pro-rata of $123,138 - $146,228 pa Level B (plus 17% employer superannuation) Join as a Research Fellow at a world top 50 University Join our collaborative research community and connect research with practice Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people transforming health The Opportunity Join the Department of Neuroscience within the School of Translational Medicine and contribute to collaborative qualitative, implementation and translational health research with hospital partners across Australia. Available at Level A or Level B, this Research Fellow opportunity will contribute to the development, implementation and evaluation of research and improvement projects, helping translate research into meaningful health system impact. The position will undertake independent and collaborative research, contributing to high-quality publications, conference presentations, research proposals and the dissemination and translation of research findings. Working across multiple sites and disciplines, the role will foster strong partnerships with hospitals, health services, clinicians and other stakeholders, while contributing to research projects, mentoring and supervision, and the development of junior researchers and postgraduate students. The position is suited to researchers at either Level A or Level B, with appointment determined by qualifications, research experience and track record. A relevant honours or doctoral qualification, or progress towards a doctorate, will be considered alongside demonstrated expertise in qualitative and/or mixed-methods research. Knowledge of implementation science, program evaluation and knowledge translation, together with strong analytical, research, communication, organisational and manuscript preparation skills, will be important. For appointment at Level B, a demonstrated capacity for independent research and a strong research track record will also be expected. If you are looking to apply your research expertise to translational health research and health system improvement, we encourage you to apply for this opportunity. Please state your preference for Level A or Level B in your cover letter as the successful applicant will be appointed commensurate with qualifications and relevant experience. This position requires a regular physical presence at the University and/or participating health service sites to support collaboration, stakeholder engagement and project delivery. The role is not designed as a primarily remote working position. Monash University seeks applicants for this opportunity who are able to demonstrate present valid and current Australian work rights. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Professor Natasha Lannin, Natasha.Lannin@monash.edu . Please only contact Natasha if you have specific inquiries regarding the position, otherwise please click on the apply button to submit your application. Position Description: Level A Research Fellow Level B Research Fellow Applications Close: Tuesday 20 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/699106/portfolio-manager-operations-reporting": {
      "title": "Portfolio Manager - Operations & Reporting",
      "description": "Portfolio Manager - Operations & Reporting Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Continuing appointment Remuneration: $140,157 - $148,769 pa HEW Level 09 (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity This is an exciting opportunity to play a pivotal role in shaping and delivering the strategic direction of a diverse University property portfolio spanning local, overseas and foundation assets. As a Portfolio Manager, you will lead strategic initiatives and projects that optimise portfolio performance, strengthen long-term property outcomes and support the University’s broader strategic priorities. Working closely with senior leaders and stakeholders across the University, you will lead and manage complex, portfolio-wide projects, bringing together strategy, financial stewardship, risk, data and performance insights to drive informed decisions and deliver tangible outcomes. You will provide trusted strategic advice, build strong stakeholder partnerships and translate portfolio priorities into practical initiatives and sustainable improvements. This is a key opportunity to lead innovation and transformation across the portfolio, including the use of artificial intelligence, automation and emerging technologies to improve decision-making, scenario planning, workflows and service delivery. You will be encouraged to challenge existing approaches, identify opportunities for strategic improvement and lead the delivery of initiatives that create meaningful value for the University. You will also build capability within the team, fostering a culture of continuous improvement, innovation and delivery. Key Selection Criteria Extensive experience in property, infrastructure or commercial portfolio oversight, including financial analysis, budgeting, reporting and senior stakeholder advice. Strong working knowledge of property management, leasing, statutory obligations, risk and compliance requirements. Highly developed analytical and conceptual capability, with the ability to interpret complex portfolio and financial information and translate insights into practical recommendations. Demonstrated interest and aptitude in AI, automation and emerging technologies , with the ability to apply these to reporting, budgeting, systems and work practices. Exceptional communication, relationship-building, influencing and negotiation skills, with the ability to engage effectively at senior levels. Outstanding planning, organisational and operational management capability, with a strong focus on priorities, accuracy, resources and delivery. Demonstrated people leadership and digital capability, including supervision, continuous improvement, Microsoft Office, property/portfolio systems and reporting tools, with a willingness to champion AI-enabled ways of working About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something groundbreaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Learn more about Monash . Be part of our story. Work with us to #ChangeIt . Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Please submit a cover letter (maximum two pages) addressing the Key Selection Criteria and outlining your suitability for the role, along with a copy of your most recent resume. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. This role requires valid work rights for Australia Position Description: Portfolio Manager - Operations & Reporting Applications Close: Sunday 25th October 2026, 11:59 PM AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698229/research-fellow": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Australian History",
      "description": "Research Fellow - Australian History Job No.: 698229 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Fixed-term, Full-time Duration: 4 years fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $123,138 - $146,228 pa Level B (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Are you a passionate historian or researcher ready to make a ground-breaking impact on Australian history? Join an esteemed team of scholars on an innovative Australian Research Council (ARC) Discovery Project dedicated to producing Australia’s first comprehensive history of disability. This dynamic full time Research Fellow position uniquely combines high-impact historical research (0.6 FTE) with vital project management (0.4 FTE), offering you the chance to drive key academic outputs while leading the operational success and public engagement of a landmark research initiative. What You Will Do Research Impact (0.6 FTE) Uncover Vital History: Conduct essential, complex historical research into Australian disability services from 1945 to the mid-1980s, filling critical gaps in scholarly knowledge. Publish and Collaborate : Lead scholarly outputs by publishing at least two peer-reviewed journal articles and contributing to a landmark co-authored monograph. Academic Community: Contribute to conference papers, research proposals, and broader academic discourse alongside leading investigators Dr Kate Murphy, Emeritus Prof. John Murphy, Emeritus Prof. Alistair Thomson, Assoc. Prof. Lorna Hallahan, and Prof. Elizabeth Tilley. Project Leadership and Management (0.4 FTE) Drive Success: Oversee day-to-day project operations to ensure seamless execution, releasing time for Chief and Partner Investigators to focus on key outputs. Event and Communication Management: Manage project correspondence and website content, and organise major public and academic events, including writing workshops and symposia. Stakeholder Engagement: Assist in executing the project’s Research Stakeholder Engagement Strategy to build strong community and sector connections. Who We Are Looking For We are seeking a collegiate, highly organised Level B Research Academic with a passion for social and historical research. Scholars with disability are particularly encouraged to apply: the selection committee will prioritise applicants with lived experience of disability. Key Qualifications and Skills Education: A Ph.D in History (or equivalent research experience). Research Expertise: Demonstrated experience in historical/scholarly research, manuscript preparation, and a strong track record of refereed publications. Project and Event Management Skills: Proven organisational abilities to run project operations, coordinate stakeholders, and organise events seamlessly. Collaborative Mindset: A strong track record of working effectively both independently and as part of an interdisciplinary research team. Communication: Exceptional written and oral communication skills, with advanced digital/computer proficiency. Why Apply? Be Part of Something Historic: Contribute directly to a national project that redefines Australia's understanding of disability, inclusion, and social history. Collaborate with Renowned Scholars: Work closely with leading academic figures in history and social policy. Develop Your Academic Career: Strengthen both your publication portfolio and high-level project management credentials. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something groundbreaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Dr. Kate Murphy, Senior Lecturer, Department of History, +61 3 9905 1184 or kate.murphy@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow - History Applications Close: Tuesday 27th October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Please submit your application as soon as possible for this fabulous opportunity to join Monash University. Interviews will be held as strong applicants are identified. Applications will close when the role has been filled. Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698709/lecturer-education-focused-frenchintercultural-studies": {
      "title": "Lecturer Education Focused Frenchintercultural Studies",
      "description": "Lecturer - French/Intercultural Studies - Education Focused Job No.: 698709 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Continuing appointment Remuneration: $123,138 - $146,228 pa Level B (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Are you an educator dedicated to shaping the future of language and intercultural education? Monash University invites dynamic academics to join our elite cohort of Education-focused staff as a Lecturer in French and Intercultural Studies . Education-focused academics at Monash are recognised as leaders in pedagogical innovation, setting the standard for exceptional design, technology-enhanced learning, and student-centred delivery. This Education-Focused Level B position brings together French-language teaching with expertise in Intercultural Studies and professional practice and leadership in intercultural settings. In both education and research terms, the work of the School of Languages, Literatures, Cultures and Linguistics centralises the cultural and political alongside the linguistic, creating capabilities and knowledge frameworks around language and communication in ways that emphasise the importance of the intercultural, the transnational and the interprofessional. The appointee will contribute to this important area of the School’s educational offer at Master’s level. The appointee will also have the capability to teach into the French language stream of the European Languages Major. The appointee will be required to have language pedagogy, technology-enhanced language learning, and second language acquisition in a classroom and online environment. Key Responsibilities Educational Leadership and Innovation: Design, deliver, and continuously refine engaging, inclusive, and technology-rich learning experiences across lectures, tutorials, and online environments. Curriculum and Assessment Design: Lead unit development, design diversified assessment strategies, and implement consistent, constructive feedback practices. Student-Centred Engagement: Foster inclusive, flexible learning spaces, drawing systematically on student feedback to drive continuous improvement. Scholarly Activity: Engage in research and scholarly activity focused on educational practice, second language acquisition, or intercultural pedagogy to inform teaching excellence. Academic Mentorship: Supervise Honours and Higher Degree by Research students and contribute actively to School, Faculty, and University-wide education initiatives. What We Are Looking For Qualifications: A PhD (or equivalent research and teaching experience) in French Studies, Applied Linguistics, Second Language Acquisition, Intercultural Studies, or a closely related field. Teaching Excellence: A proven track record of inspiring, effective teaching and assessment delivery in a higher education environment. Language proficiency: Demonstrated high-level proficiency in French and the capacity to teach French language across the relevant undergraduate levels. Pedagogical Expertise: Demonstrated capability in curriculum development, technology-enhanced language learning, and evidence-informed pedagogical approaches. Intercultural Focus: Ability to teach French language alongside intercultural and professional leadership practice in diverse settings. Collaborative Leadership: Strong interpersonal, organisation, and team leadership skills, with a commitment to student success and academic innovation. Why Monash? At Monash, we value educators who challenge the status quo. As an Education-Focused Level B academic, you will receive targeted support on a clear trajectory toward educational leadership within one of Australia’s top research-intensive universities. You will work in a supportive, forward-thinking environment that centralizes the cultural, political, and linguistic dynamics of today's interconnected world. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Professor Jo Winning, Head of Languages, Literature, Culture and Linguistics, +61 3 9905 2945 or jo.winning@monash.edu Position Description: Lecturer - Education Focused Applications Close: Monday 2nd November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Please submit your application as soon as possible for this fabulous opportunity to join Monash University. Interviews will be held as strong applicants are identified. Applications will close when the role has been filled. Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/696505/indigenous-research-fellow-identified": {
      "title": "Indigenous Research Fellow Identified",
      "description": "Indienous Research Fellow (Identified) Job No.: 696505 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Part-time, 0.6 for years 1 and 2 and 0.8 for year 3 Duration: 3 year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $123,138 - $146,228 pa Level B (pro rata) plus 17% employer superannuation Identified role. Open to Indigenous applicants only Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Pursuant to a \"special measure\" under section 12 of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic), in order to promote and help realise substantive equality for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islanders, this position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander applicants only. Join the School of Education and Culture as an Indigenous Research Fellow and play a leading role in the Australian Research Council (ARC) Discovery Project, Hitting the Limits: Intersectional Sexisms in Australian Universities. Working closely with a national research team, you will lead and undertake research with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander women and gender diverse academics across Australian universities, applying Indigenous knowledges, methodologies and culturally informed approaches throughout all stages of the research process. In this role, you will design and deliver high-quality research, engage collaboratively with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander participants, communities and organisations, and ensure the research is conducted in culturally safe, ethical and relational ways. You will contribute to data collection, analysis, interpretation and dissemination, producing high-quality publications and presentations while supporting the project's aim of improving outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. You will also contribute to the broader research environment through developing funding proposals, supervising research support staff and higher degree research students, mentoring junior researchers, undertaking research-related administration, participating in professional activities and conferences, and making occasional contributions to teaching within your area of expertise. As the successful candidate, you will hold a doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline or demonstrate equivalent research experience, with a strong track record of research publications and experience conducting qualitative and/or quantitative research. You will have demonstrated experience undertaking research with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities using Indigenous methodologies and culturally responsive approaches, together with a strong understanding of Indigenous Data Sovereignty, Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property, relational accountability and ethical research practice. You will be a collaborative and organised researcher with excellent communication skills, able to work independently and within an interdisciplinary team. You will bring experience supervising or mentoring researchers and/or higher degree research students, building respectful relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and organisations, and a strong understanding of the contemporary experiences of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples within Australian higher education. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Associate Professor Emily Gray, School of Education and Culture, emily.gray2@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow (Identified) Applications Close: Monday 16th November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
