    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698026/research-fellow-digital-civil-justice": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Digital Civil Justice",
      "description": "Research Fellow - Digital Civil Justice Job No.: 698026 Location: Clayton campus and Monash University Law Chambers Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 12 Month fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity An exciting opportunity exists for an outstanding early career scholar to join the Faculty of Law. The Monash Law Faculty was established over 60 years ago to be a different kind of law school, with a pioneering spirit, an enterprising and inclusive approach, and a deep commitment to access to justice in all of its forms. We aim to produce talented legal thinkers who have strong ethical, forensic and practical legal skills that can be applied to creatively solve real-world problems. We invest in premium facilities worthy of our world-class students and staff, such as our renowned clinical sites, state-of-the art moot court, and contemporary, student-centred learning environments. The Research Fellow will be based in the Warren Centre for Civil Justice. As the successful candidate you will work with a team on Redesigning Digital Justice, an ARC Discovery Project on improving the design methods and technologies used in online hearings. The project brings together leading Law and Human-Computer Interaction researchers at Monash University. The project aims to bring advances in human-computer interaction to the forefront of online justice systems. We will centre the needs and experiences of litigants in designing effective systems and processes and developing a set of design principles, methods, and technologies for online hearings. This work tackles the challenge of creating systems consistent with legal standards acceptable to justice institutions and actors and inclusive of stakeholders, particularly people with disabilities. To be appointable into this role you must hold a degree in law and a doctoral qualification in the relevant discipline area (e.g. law, social science, sociology) or equivalent qualifications or research experience and demonstrated expertise in civil justice research, particularly in relation to such topics as self-represented litigants and/or technology in courts and tribunals. There is flexibility in the program to accommodate a range of different skills and experiences the Research Fellow might bring, including but not limited to qualitative research experience; experience in co-production, co-design, human-centred design or participatory research methods; and legal practising experience relevant to civil justice, particularly in connection with court and/or tribunal hearings. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Professor and Director, Warren Centre for Civil Justice - genevieve.grant@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow Applications Close: 30 October 2026 11:55pm AEDT Please submit your application as soon as possible for this fabulous opportunity to join Monash University. Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/697933/research-assistant-neurosymbolic-reasoning": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Neuro-Symbolic Reasoning",
      "description": "Research Assistant - Neuro-Symbolic Reasoning Job No.: 697933 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Part-time, fraction (0.5) Duration: 6 month fixed-term appointment Remuneration: Pro-rata of $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact as a Research Assistant at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community advancing trustworthy AI Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Join a research program advancing trustworthy AI collaboration through the development of novel neuro-symbolic AI methods for reasoning over multi-agent interactions. This Research Assistant position will contribute to research exploring how temporal knowledge representation, abductive and probabilistic reasoning and machine learning can be used to understand complex behaviours such as reliability, competence, transparency and trust. The position will design and develop innovative reasoning algorithms and knowledge-based, probabilistic and neuro-symbolic frameworks integrated with machine learning and reinforcement learning. The role will contribute to high-impact research publications, research software, experimental frameworks and evaluation methodologies. We are seeking someone with a PhD or near completion in Artificial Intelligence, Computer Science, Machine Learning or a closely related discipline, together with strong research expertise in areas such as neuro-symbolic AI, knowledge representation and reasoning, temporal reasoning, probabilistic or differentiable logic, graph-based machine learning, knowledge graphs or multi-agent reinforcement learning. Strong programming skills in Python and AI frameworks such as PyTorch, JAX or TensorFlow, combined with excellent analytical, problem-solving, communication, organisational and project management skills, will also support success in this role. Join a collaborative research environment developing explainable and trustworthy AI systems for collaborative decision-making and apply for this exciting Research Fellow opportunity. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Dr. Naim Rastgoo, Senior Research Fellow, Department of Data Science and Artificial Intelligence, naim.rastgoo@monash.edu Position Description: Research Assistant Applications Close: Thursday 29th October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/699020/monashbayreuth-double-phd-degree-opportunity-international-research-training-group-optexc-optical-excitations-in-organic-and-inorganic-semiconductors": {
      "title": "Monash/Bayreuth Double PhD Degree Opportunity – International Research Training Group “OPTEXC” - Optical Excitations in Organic and Inorganic Semiconductors",
      "description": "Monash/Bayreuth Double PhD Degree Opportunity – International Research Training Group “OPTEXC” - Optical Excitations in Organic and Inorganic Semiconductors Job No.: 699020 Locations: Clayton [Home] / Bayreuth [Host] Study mode: Full-time Degrees: E8009.1 - Doctor of Philosophy (Monash - Bayreuth) Duration: 3.5 years fixed-term appointment Remuneration: AUD $37,145 per annum (tax-free stipend, 2026 full-time rate, pro-rata) About the Monash–Bayreuth Joint PhD Program The Doctor of Philosophy (Monash – Bayreuth) is an elite, international double-degree doctoral program delivered through a collaborative partnership between Monash University (Australia) and the University of Bayreuth (Germany). Designed to train the next generation of global research leaders, the program enables candidates to conduct cutting-edge research under joint supervision from both world-class institutions, culminating in a recognized double PhD degree. Monash University (Australia): Ranked 31st globally and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight, Monash is a powerhouse of research innovation. Its Department of Materials Science and Engineering ranks second nationally, driving breakthroughs in advanced materials, polymer chemistry, biomaterials, and metallurgy within state-of-the-art facilities. University of Bayreuth (Germany): A renown center for interdisciplinary excellence, Bayreuth is globally recognized for its pioneering research in optical excitations, organic/inorganic semiconductors, macromolecular science, and advanced energy technologies. The International Joint Degree: As part of this program (integrated within the DFG-funded IRTG 2818 / OPTEXC framework), candidates complete an extended research stay of 12 months at the partner institution. Doctoral researchers gain access to cross-continental industry links, specialized facilities, and dual academic networks, graduating with two doctoral degrees to maximize global career impact. The research opportunity Monash University has partnered with the University of Bayreuth (Germany) along with the University of Melbourne to constitute an interdisciplinary International Research Training Group (IRTG): “Optical excitations in organic and inorganic semiconductors” ( https://irtg-optexc.de/ ). As part of this program there is the opportunity for students to undertake fully funded joint degrees with the University of Bayreuth in Germany in the areas of experimental and theoretical physics, synthetic, physical and computational chemistry, material sciences and related areas. The OPTEXC IRTG involves 20 academics in Australia and Germany working in complementary areas related to novel semiconductor materials. A range of PhD projects are on offer relating to understanding how the optoelectronic properties of semiconductors, notably the nature of their photoexcitations, can be tailored by employing external stimuli such as local fields. In this IRTG, you will be part of an international and diverse team working on both organic and inorganic semiconductors, in order to develop a general understanding of the effects of external stimuli on the photophysical properties of these types of semiconductors. This IRTG provides an outstanding environment for research training and a unique opportunity to undertake a truly international PhD, with a one-year research stay at the University of Bayreuth in Germany part of the doctoral qualification program, leading to joint PhDs with degrees from both universities. Applications are sought from outstanding candidates with backgrounds in chemistry, physics, materials science and related disciplines. Specific projects seeking applications are: Autonomous experimental solar cell materials discovery. Supervisor: Prof. Udo Bach, Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering. (Email: udo.bach@monash.edu) Next-generation printable solar cells Supervisor: Prof. Jacek Jasieniak, Department of Materials Science and Engineering. (Email: jacek.asieniak@monash.edu) Advanced in-situ synchrotron X-ray characterisation of solution-processed semiconductor films Supervisor: Prof. Chris McNeill, Department of Materials Science and Engineering (Email: christopher.mcneill@monash.edu ) For further information about the OPTEXC IRTG in general, please consult the website: https://irtg-optexc.de/ . For specific questions about the research projects above, please email the corresponding supervisor. What makes this opportunity special? Earn a Doctor of Philosophy from Monash University and the University of Bayreuth (Germany). 12-month research stay in Germany - Undertake a fully funded 12-month research stay at the University of Bayreuth as part of the program. Be part of a larger student cohort with joint workshops and training opportunities. Who are we looking for? We are particularly interested in applicants with a background or experience in: Materials Science and Engineering Physics Chemistry Successful applicants will also demonstrate: Excellent academic and research capability Strong written and verbal English communication skills Strong problem-solving and communication skills The ability to work independently and as part of a multidisciplinary research team. Qualifications: Applicants should hold, or be close to completing, a Bachelor Honours degree (H1 or equivalent) and/or Master's degree with an excellent academic record. Candidates currently completing a 4-year Honours degree (or equivalent 3+1 year program) with a final-year research project, aiming for or achieving an H1 standard, will also be considered. Applicants who already hold a PhD are not eligible . This opportunity is open to international applicants. Degree Information E8009.1 - Doctor of Philosophy (Monash - Bayreuth) Application Process Please submit your application to Prof. Chris McNeill ( christopher.mcneill@monash.edu ) which should include: CV All academic transcripts Proof of English language proficiency if relevant (see https://www.monash.edu/graduate-research/study/apply/english-language-proficiency-requirements-for-admission) Cover letter nominating one of the projects listed above and what skills you would bring to the project. Shortlisted applications will be invited to a video interview. Enquiries Prof. Chris McNeill: christopher.mcneill@monash.edu Applications close: 30 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT or earlier if the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed as they are received, and early applications are encouraged. Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698218/senior-lecturer-practice-monash-synthetic-biology-platform": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer (Practice), Monash Synthetic Biology Platform",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer (Practice), Monash Synthetic Biology Platform Job No.: 698218 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 2-year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $150,841 - $173,931 pa Level C (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact as Senior Lecturer (Practice) in synthetic biology Join our collaborative community advancing protein and peptide discovery Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas in protein design and innovation The Opportunity Join the Monash Synthetic Biology Platform (MSBP), a newly developed research technology Platform advancing the design and development of novel proteins and peptides through the integration of in silico protein design, protein chemistry, liquid handling and functional screening. The Platform brings together complementary capabilities to enable discovery and development across the Faculty, University and external academic and industry sectors, with applications spanning new medicines, diagnostics and advanced manufacturing. As Senior Lecturer (Practice), you will develop and implement workflows for protein and peptide expression, integrating AI-mediated protein design pipelines and contributing to research and industry projects involving the design and screening of new-to-nature proteins and peptides. You will provide specialist advice across MSBP and the Faculty of Medicine, Nursing and Health Sciences, contribute to scopes of work and complex data analysis, and collaborate with capabilities in protein expression, crystallography and high-throughput functional genetic screening. The role will also support Platform growth through technical expertise, client engagement and promotion of its advanced capabilities. We are seeking someone with a PhD or relevant Master’s qualification with equivalent professional experience, and substantial experience in protein or peptide biology and discovery research. Knowledge of protein expression, screening and functional characterisation, high-throughput molecular biology, robotic workflows and computational protein design will be highly relevant, together with research innovation, industry or professional contribution and subject matter expertise. Strong project management, mentoring, communication and relationship-building skills will support effective collaboration with researchers, students, industry partners and other stakeholders. Join the Faculty and contribute to an exciting research environment at the intersection of synthetic biology, protein design and advanced research technologies—we look forward to receiving your application. Monash University seeks applicants for this opportunity who are able to demonstrate present valid and current Australian work rights. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Professor Ian Smyth, Associate Dean, Research and Research Infrastructure, Ian.Smyth@monash.edu Position Description: Senior Lecturer (Practice) Applications Close: Sunday 25 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698679/research-fellow-computational-microbiome-scientist": {
      "title": "Research Fellow (Computational Microbiome Scientist)",
      "description": "Research Fellow (Computational Microbiome Scientist) Job No.: 698679 Location: Box Hill Hospital Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 2-year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University through microbiome research Join our inclusive, collaborative community of computational researchers Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people advancing cancer research The Opportunity Join the Gunjur Laboratory as a Research Fellow (Computational Microbiome Scientist) and contribute to research investigating the relationship between the human-associated microbiome, diet and cancer outcomes. Working across prospective clinical cohorts, biospecimens, shotgun metagenomics and large public datasets, the position will work closely with the principal investigator to support a growing computational microbiome research program. The position will develop, implement and apply bioinformatics approaches to analyse shotgun metagenomic sequencing and other -omics datasets from laboratory and clinical research studies. Key activities include establishing reproducible computational workflows, contributing to study design and data curation, undertaking statistical analyses and data integration, and generating publication-quality figures, reports and presentations. The position will also contribute to manuscript preparation, grant applications and the supervision and training of research students. We are seeking someone with an honours degree and/or progress towards or completion of a doctorate in a relevant or closely related field. Strong analytical and manuscript preparation skills, experience analysing complex clinical or biological datasets and the ability to develop well-documented code in R, Python or another bioinformatics language are required, along with experience analysing shotgun metagenomic data. Knowledge of other -omics analysis, Unix-based systems and high-performance computing will also support success in this role, together with strong communication, organisational and collaborative skills. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a growing area of microbiome and cancer research, and we look forward to receiving your application. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Dr Ashray Gunjur, Senior Lecturer & Laboratory Head, Ashray.Gunjur@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow Applications Close: Friday 6 November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
