    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698530/research-fellow": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Research Fellow Job No.: 698530 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 3 year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity A rare and exciting opportunity exists to get involved in high-impact research in evolutionary physiology at the School of Biological Sciences alongside world-class researchers. You will have the opportunity to design and undertake research, focussed on understanding the evolutionary drivers and ecological consequences of variation in metabolic rate, life history, and thermal tolerance in animals (primarily insects). The successful incumbent will undertake measurement of metabolic rate using high-throughput flow-through respirometry, use artificial selection to create replicate populations of organisms that differ in their life history, and phenotype the physiological, ecological, behavioural, and life history traits of the resultant populations. The patterns identified through this endeavour will be compared with patterns observed in comparative data sets compiled from the literature, analysed using phylogenetically informed statistical approaches. As the successful candidate you will hold a doctoral qualification in the relevant discipline or a closely related field and have some experience and possess a demonstrated capacity in one or more of: the measurement of metabolic rate of animals using direct or indirect calorimetry, experimental evolution studies, phylogenetically informed statistical analysis of comparative data compiled from the literature and/or collected using experimental studies, and the ability to develop and implement evolutionary models. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Professor and Head, School of Biological Sciences - craig.white@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow Applications Close: Friday 30 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Please submit your application as soon as possible for this fabulous opportunity to join Monash University. Interviews will be held as strong applicants are identified. Applications will close when the role has been filled. Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698934/fitness-instructor": {
      "title": "Fitness Instructor",
      "description": "Fitness Instructor Job No.: 698934 Location: Clayton, Caulfield and Peninsula campus Employment Type: Casual Duration: 16-week casual engagement Remuneration: $45.03 per hour, HEW Level 03 Casual loaded rate plus applicable penalty rates for evenings and weekends Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Monash Sport has exciting opportunities to join the team as a Fitness Instructor across our Clayton, Caulfield, and Peninsula campuses. As the successful candidate, you will be responsible for providing a range of fitness-based services to the Monash Sport community, including but not limited to provision of core services to members, including taking a professional and innovative approach to individual program design and diligent and proactive supervision of the Fitness Centre. At Peninsula and Caulfield campus you will also be required to provide a high level of customer service across a wide range of Service Desk offerings and functions, including (but not limited to) membership sales and renewals, Point of Sales (POS) transactions, and casual court hire bookings. Health and Fitness Instructor essential requirements: Certificate III and Certificate IV in Fitness or Tertiary Qualification (relevant in fitness/exercise field) Current CPR and Level 2 First Aid Certificate Current accredited level registration with AUSactive About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something groundbreaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Learn more about Monash . Join the pursuit of our purpose to build a better future for ourselves and our communities - #ChangeIt with us. To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: For opportunities at the Clayton Campus, Malcolm Hatfield, Fitness Operations Coordinator, 03 9905 1160 For opportunities at the Caulfield Campus, Summer Beal, Site Coordinator, 03 9904 4413 For opportunities at the Peninsula Campus, Shane Adams, Site Coordinator, 03 9904 4497 Position Description: Fitness Instructor Applications Close: Sunday 18 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698995/doctor-of-philosophy-phd-in-machine-learning-for-in-situ-materials-characterisation": {
      "title": "Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) in Machine Learning for In Situ Materials Characterisation",
      "description": "Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) in Machine Learning for In Situ Materials Characterisation Job No.: 698995 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Graduate Research Degree: 3291 - Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) Hiring: 2027, flexible by agreement Duration: 3.5-year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: AUD 41,100 per annum (tax-free stipend) (2027 rate) Scholarship status: Fully funded PhD scholarship available for an outstanding domestic or international candidate. Monash Engineering is a leading global centre for research consistently placing highly in international ranking lists. We engage with industry and other world leading institutes to carry out pioneering research that benefits society and changes the world around us. Our world-class researchers are driven by a passion and commitment to leaving a more sustainable legacy for future generations. We are committed to training the next generation of research leaders through our graduate research degrees. Our students have the opportunity to make an impact and solve real world issues in a stimulating, supportive environment with access to cutting edge facilities. The Opportunity Applications are invited from outstanding candidates for PhD study in the Department of Materials Science and Engineering within the Faculty of Engineering. Project title: Machine Learning for In Situ Materials Characterisation. We are seeking an outstanding PhD candidate to develop machine learning methods for advanced and in situ materials characterisation. Modern characterisation techniques generate increasingly complex datasets describing microstructure, crystallography, phase evolution, chemistry and morphology. This project will investigate how machine learning can learn physically meaningful representations directly from these data and help understand how materials evolve during processing and phase transformation. The candidate will work with experimental datasets from techniques such as electron microscopy, EBSD, X-ray diffraction, X-ray imaging and in situ characterisation. Depending on the candidate's background and research direction, approaches may include computer vision, representation learning, graph neural networks, multimodal learning and scientific machine learning. A central aim is to develop quantitative relationships linking processing, material evolution, microstructure and properties. Applications will focus primarily on metals, phase transformations and materials processing, including emerging low-emission metallurgical processes. The candidate will be supervised by Dr Yuxiang Wu ( yuxiang.wu@monash.edu ) in a multidisciplinary environment spanning materials science, advanced characterisation, computational modelling and artificial intelligence. Project Details Start: 2027, flexible by agreement Graduate research degree: Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) Location: Clayton campus Department: Materials Science and Engineering Fully funded PhD scholarship available for an outstanding domestic or international candidate, subject to formal Monash assessment and approvals. Preferred Background Materials Science, Metallurgical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Physics, Computer Science, Data Science, Chemical Engineering, or related disciplines. Strong interest in machine learning, materials characterisation or computational materials science. Previous experience with machine learning, computer vision, graph neural networks, Python, SEM/EBSD, XRD, image analysis, microstructure characterisation or materials modelling is desirable. Candidate Requirements Applicants will be considered provided they fulfil the criteria for PhD admission at Monash University. Details of the relevant requirements are available at www.monash.edu/engineering/future-students/graduate-research/how-to-apply . Your application will be looked upon favourably if you: Graduated in the top 10% of your year level. Graduated from a well ranked university. Have authored peer-reviewed research publications. Possess excellent written and verbal English skills. Note: applicants who already hold a PhD degree will not be considered. Applicants must show strong quantitative and problem-solving skills, excellent communication and teamwork skills, and the capacity to conduct self-motivated research. Candidates are not expected to already be experts in both machine learning and materials science; strong candidates from either background who are interested in working across the interface are encouraged to apply. Application Process To apply for a graduate research degree and/or scholarship at Monash Engineering please follow the following steps: Check your eligibility, including the PhD entry requirements and Monash's English Language Proficiency requirements. You may contact Dr Yuxiang Wu by email with your CV, academic transcript, and a brief statement outlining your interest in the project. Submit a full application through My.App at myapp.monash.edu/s , listing Dr Yuxiang Wu as your preferred supervisor. You are not required to submit an Expression of Interest or receive an invitation to apply before submitting your My.App application. Enquiries For enquiries about the project, please contact Dr Yuxiang Wu at yuxiang.wu@monash.edu . Further enquiries about the scholarship or application process should be directed to the Graduate Research Office at eng-gradresearch@monash.edu or visit www.monash.edu/engineering/future-students/graduate-research . Applications Close: Friday 30 April 2027, 11:55pm AEST Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698996/doctor-of-philosophy-phd-in-physical-metallurgy-of-hydrogenbased-iron-oxide-reduction": {
      "title": "Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) in Physical Metallurgy of Hydrogen-Based Iron Oxide Reduction",
      "description": "Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) in Physical Metallurgy of Hydrogen-Based Iron Oxide Reduction Job No.: 698996 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Graduate Research Degree: 3291 - Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) Hiring: 2027, flexible by agreement Duration: 3.5-year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: AUD 41,100 per annum (tax-free stipend) (2027 rate) Scholarship status: Funded PhD project for domestic and international applicants, subject to Monash eligibility and competitive selection. Monash Engineering is a leading global centre for research consistently placing highly in international ranking lists. We engage with industry and other world leading institutes to carry out pioneering research that benefits society and changes the world around us. Our world-class researchers are driven by a passion and commitment to leaving a more sustainable legacy for future generations. We are committed to training the next generation of research leaders through our graduate research degrees. Our students have the opportunity to make an impact and solve real world issues in a stimulating, supportive environment with access to cutting edge facilities. The Opportunity Applications are invited from outstanding candidates for PhD study in the Department of Materials Science and Engineering within the Faculty of Engineering. Project title: Physical Metallurgy of Hydrogen-Based Iron Oxide Reduction. We are seeking an outstanding PhD candidate to investigate the physical metallurgy of hydrogen-based iron oxide reduction, with applications to low-carbon and green ironmaking. The project will investigate how phase transformations, reduction pathways and microstructure evolution interact during the conversion of iron oxides to metallic iron. Particular emphasis will be placed on how temperature, gas atmosphere, starting microstructure and ore chemistry influence reduction kinetics, porosity and the morphology of the resulting iron. The candidate will combine high-temperature reduction experiments with advanced materials characterisation and modelling. Techniques may include thermogravimetric analysis, in situ X-ray diffraction, SEM, EBSD, image analysis, thermodynamic calculations and kinetic modelling. The aim is to develop a microstructure-informed physical framework for iron oxide reduction, moving beyond purely empirical descriptions of reduction kinetics. The candidate will be supervised by Dr Yuxiang Wu ( yuxiang.wu@monash.edu ) in a multidisciplinary environment spanning physical metallurgy, advanced characterisation, computational modelling and green ironmaking. Project Details Start: 2027, flexible by agreement Graduate research degree: Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) Location: Clayton campus Department: Materials Science and Engineering Funded PhD project for domestic and international applicants, subject to Monash eligibility, competitive selection and formal approvals. Preferred Background Materials Science, Metallurgical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Chemical Engineering, Physics, or related disciplines. Strong interest in physical metallurgy, thermodynamics, kinetics and phase transformations. Previous experience with phase transformations, SEM/EBSD, XRD, thermal analysis, modelling or scientific programming is desirable. Candidate Requirements Applicants will be considered provided they fulfil the criteria for PhD admission at Monash University. Details of the relevant requirements are available at www.monash.edu/engineering/future-students/graduate-research/how-to-apply . Your application will be looked upon favourably if you: Graduated in the top 10% of your year level. Graduated from a well ranked university. Have authored peer-reviewed research publications. Possess excellent written and verbal English skills. Note: applicants who already hold a PhD degree will not be considered. Applicants must show strong communication and problem-solving skills and the capacity to conduct independent, quantitative research. They should have either completed, or be in the process of completing, a Bachelors H1 Honours Degree or already hold an H1E Bachelors and/or Masters degree. Candidates who are in the process of completing their H1 degree will be considered. Application Process To apply for a graduate research degree and/or scholarship at Monash Engineering please follow the following steps: Check your eligibility, including the PhD entry requirements and Monash's English Language Proficiency requirements. You may contact Dr Yuxiang Wu by email with your CV, academic transcript, and a brief statement outlining your interest in the project. Submit a full application through My.App at myapp.monash.edu/s , listing Dr Yuxiang Wu as your preferred supervisor. You are not required to submit an Expression of Interest or receive an invitation to apply before submitting your My.App application. Enquiries For enquiries about the project, please contact Dr Yuxiang Wu at yuxiang.wu@monash.edu . Further enquiries about the scholarship or application process should be directed to the Graduate Research Office at eng-gradresearch@monash.edu or visit www.monash.edu/engineering/future-students/graduate-research . Applications Close: Friday 30 April 2027, 11:55pm AEST Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/697788/research-assistant-in-respiratory-chronic-disease-management": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Respiratory & Chronic Disease Management",
      "description": "Research Assistant in Respiratory & Chronic Disease Management Job No.: 697788 Location: The Alfred Centre Employment Type: Part-time, fraction (0.8) - Full-time (1.0) Duration: Fixed-term appointment until 31 December 2027 Remuneration: Pro-rata of $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) Make your mark in research at a world top 50 University Join our collaborative community and help shape better health outcomes Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Join the Respiratory Access (Cox) Group and contribute to NHMRC-funded research focused on chronic lung disease and supportive care. This Research Assistant position will support a range of clinical research activities, including quantitative and qualitative data collection, clinical trial interventions, participant recruitment and consenting, data management and analysis, systematic reviews and preparation of research outputs. Working within established research objectives, protocols and timeframes, the position will contribute to research that supports improved understanding and care for people living with chronic lung disease. Working closely with clinicians, researchers, and patients, you will contribute to the design and delivery of clinical research that directly impacts real-world care. This role is ideally suited to an early career researcher or health sciences graduate with experience in clinical data collection and trial coordination. This is a unique opportunity to grow your research experience in a high-impact, patient-focused environment, supported by a collaborative team at the cutting edge of respiratory health care. What you’ll do: Collect and manage clinical trial data (quantitative and qualitative) Deliver trial interventions and assist with patient-facing studies Contribute to ethics submissions, manuscript preparation, and data analysis Assist in systematic reviews and grant development What you’ll bring: An honours degree or higher in a biomedical, allied health, or clinical research field Experience working in hospital or clinical research environments Excellent written and verbal communication skills Demonstrated knowledge of Good Clinical Practice (ICH-GCP) Strong organisational, documentation, and data management skills Join a collaborative research environment and apply your clinical research expertise to support impactful chronic disease management research outcomes—we look forward to receiving your application. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground-breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Join the pursuit of our purpose to build a better future for ourselves and our communities - #ChangeIt with us. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Associate Professor Narelle Cox, Principal Research Fellow, Narelle.Cox@monash.edu. Please only contact Narelle if you have specific inquiries regarding the position, otherwise please click on the apply button to submit your application. Position Description: Research Assistant Applications Close: Wednesday 14 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/697453/media-and-communications-adviser": {
      "title": "Media and Communications Adviser",
      "description": "Media and Communications Adviser Job No.: 697453 Location: Mulgrave Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term from December 2026 until September 2027 Remuneration: $110,527 – $121,227 pa HEW 07 (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Join Monash University as our next Media and Communications Adviser and play a leading role in shaping and amplifying the University’s global narrative. This is a unique opportunity to position one of the world’s leading universities at the forefront of media and public conversation. Working within the University Communications Division, you will implement corporate communications strategies that bring the University’s story to life, aligned with Focus Monash and its research priorities. As a trusted advisor, you will provide clear and timely guidance to senior stakeholders in a fast paced environment. You will lead proactive and reactive media engagement, manage enquiries, and strengthen relationships with key media and internal partners to maximise impact. In this role, you will: Deliver integrated media and communications activity across channels Promote the University’s achievements through strategic media engagement and content development Support divisional and University wide communications priorities Use research and sentiment analysis to inform decisions Uphold brand, risk and compliance standards Build strong partnerships with senior leaders and stakeholders Drive continuous improvement across media operations This position is ideal for a dynamic communications professional with demonstrated expertise in media liaison and public relations management, exceptional written and verbal communication skills, and the ability to provide strategic advice at the highest levels. You will thrive in a collaborative environment, balancing strategic thinking with hands-on execution, and delivering results to tight deadlines. If you are motivated by purpose, driven by impact and ready to contribute to a globally recognised institution’s reputation and influence, this is your opportunity to make a meaningful difference. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. This role requires current valid work rights for Australia. For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must include your latest CV and a Cover Letter (max 2 pages) addressing the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries : Toni Brient, Media and Communications Manager - Toni.Brient@Monash.edu / 0423 964 191 Position Description : Media and Communications Adviser Applications Close : Monday 19 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698874/phd-scholarship-opportunity-unravelling-cancer-metabolite-recognition-by-t-cells": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship Opportunity - Unravelling Cancer Metabolite Recognition by T Cells",
      "description": "PhD Scholarship Opportunity - Unravelling Cancer Metabolite Recognition by T Cells Job No.: 698874 Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 3.5-year fixed-term appointment Location: Monash University, Clayton campus, Melbourne, Australia Remuneration: The successful applicant will receive a tax-free stipend, at the current value of ~ $41,100 per annum 2027 full-time rate, as per the Monash Research Training Program (RTP) Stipend https://www.monash.edu/study/fees-scholarships/scholarships/find-a-scholarship/research-training-program-scholarship#scholarship-details . Be inspired, every day Drive your own learning at one of the world’s top 50 universities Take your career in exciting, rewarding directions A fully funded PhD scholarship is available in the group of Dr Wael Awad within Prof. Jamie Rossjohn’s laboratory at the Monash Biomedicine Discovery Institute (BDI). This research proposal focuses on providing a holistic understanding of how cancer-associated metabolites orchestrate T cell functions. Utilizing a combination of cutting-edge biochemical, structural, and cellular immunology approaches, the successful candidate will investigate the molecular mechanisms underlying T cell receptor recognition and discrimination of tumour metabolites. This fundamental research aims to pave the way for universal MR1-based anti-cancer immunotherapies. The successful candidate gains hands-on expertise in recombinant protein expression and purification, X-ray crystallography, Surface Plasmon Resonance (SPR), and cellular immunology approaches in a world-class, exceptionally well-funded environment. The candidate will join a world-leading structural immunology research team with a proven track record of publishing in high-impact journals including Science, Nature Immunology, and Science Immunology. The position offers an exceptionally well-funded environment, state-of-the-art facilities, direct mentorship from world leaders, and opportunities to present at national and international conferences. Eligibility requirements: Candidates will need to fulfil the Monash University minimum requirements for admission to a PhD detailed here: https://www.monash.edu/admissions/entry-requirements/minimum It is expected that applicants will have a background in biochemistry, structural biology, and/or immunology and knowledge of and/or experience in protein biochemistry, cellular biology and/or structural characterization. The project is based in Melbourne, Australia. The candidate can commence the project in January 2027; however alternative arrangements can be made. How to apply Please submit a single package containing: A cover letter outlining your research interests and motivation for a PhD (maximum 1 page). A CV including qualifications, achievements, publications, work history, and two references. A copy of your academic transcript(s). Please submit your application via email to wael.awad@monash.edu with the subject line \"PhD Application – MR1 Structural Immunology” Applications Close: Tuesday 18 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698489/postdoctoral-research-fellow-chemical-biological-engineering": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Chemical and Biological Engineering",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Chemical and Biological Engineering Job No.: 698489 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 2 year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Are you a driven materials researcher looking to pioneer the next wave of scientific discovery? Join the Faculty of Engineering at Monash University as a Research Fellow (Level A) and help to lead the charge in discovering, fabricating, and optimising emerging 2D halide perovskite materials. Positioned at the dynamic intersection of materials science, optoelectronics, laboratory automation, and data science, this role offers an extraordinary platform to elevate your research career within a world-class, collaborative environment. Why You Should Apply Be Part of a Major National Collaboration: Work directly within a newly funded ARC Discovery Project and an ACAP-funded High-Throughput Material Discovery Platform. You will bridge nodes across leading Australian institutions, including Monash University, the University of Melbourne, RMIT, UNSW, and the University of Sydney, building an exceptional professional network. Pioneer Data-Driven Science: Combine hands-on, experimental materials discovery with cutting-edge high-throughput platforms, robotics, machine learning, and autonomous experimental workflows. Access World-Class Facilities: Based in the Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering at Monash, you will utilise state-of-the-art photophysical and optoelectronic characterisation suites. About the Role As a Level A Research Fellow, you will contribute directly to defined research projects while building your independent academic profile. Key Responsibilities: Investigate the growth, physical properties, thin films, and optoelectronic device development of 2D metal halide perovskites (including single-crystalline microcrystals). Apply advanced photophysical techniques such as steady-state/time-resolved photoluminescence, Raman spectroscopy, transient absorption spectroscopy, and electrical measurements. Operate and develop high-throughput fabrication platforms utilising advanced data processing, programming, and machine learning/automation approaches. Publish high-impact research findings and present results across key academic and industry forums. Collaborate seamlessly across a multidisciplinary team while engaging in light academic, committee, and research-led teaching opportunities. What We Are Looking For Education: A Ph.D. in Materials Science, Chemistry, Physics, Chemical Engineering, or a closely related discipline. Core Expertise: A strong research track record in functional materials, with specific hands-on experience in metal halide perovskites (particularly 2D perovskites), crystal growth, thin-film fabrication, or optoelectronic devices. Analytical Skills: Deep knowledge of relevant optoelectronic and photophysical characterisation techniques including Raman spectroscopy, (time-correlated) photoluminescence microscopy, transient absorption spectroscopy and electrical measurements. Desirable Edge: High-throughput experimentation, laboratory automation, programming, robotics, machine learning, or autonomous experimental design experience will be highly regarded. Team Mindset: Excellent written and verbal communication skills with a passion for interdisciplinary team success. We are particularly interested in candidates who are enthusiastic about combining experimental materials research with emerging data-driven and autonomous approaches to accelerate materials discovery. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Professor Udo Bach, Chemical and Biological Engineering, udo.bach@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow - CBE Applications Close: Tuesday 13th October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Please submit your application as soon as possible for this fabulous opportunity to join Monash University. Interviews will be held as strong applicants are identified. Applications will close when the role has been filled. Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/697887/business-analysis-and-improvement-officer": {
      "title": "Business Analysis and Improvement Officer",
      "description": "Business Analysis and Improvement Officer Job No.: 697887 Location: 211 Wellington Road, Mulgrave Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term appointment until 23 August 2027 Remuneration: HEW 7 ($110,527 - $121,227) plus 17% superannuation Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Join the Finance portfolio as a Business Analysis and Improvement Officer and lead complex analytics, process improvement and change initiatives that support operational excellence across the University. You’ll play a key role in identifying opportunities to streamline, digitise and automate financial processes, while delivering insightful analytics through data extraction, reporting and visualisation to support informed decision-making. Working within the Finance Applications and Processes team, you’ll lead continuous improvement initiatives, business requirements gathering and process rationalisation, as well as contribute to projects involving system development and testing. You’ll work with a broad range of stakeholders to improve efficiency, reduce costs and enhance the quality and effectiveness of Finance services. About You You’ll be an analytical and solutions-focused professional with demonstrated experience in business analysis, data analytics and process improvement. You’ll bring strong technical capability across data extraction and transformation, visualisation, database querying and automation, with experience using tools such as Python and process mapping software. You’ll have a proven ability to lead change and continuous improvement initiatives that deliver tangible business outcomes, combined with excellent organisational, communication and stakeholder management skills. You’ll be confident providing specialist advice, influencing senior stakeholders and translating complex information into practical solutions that improve processes, systems and service delivery. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something groundbreaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Learn more about Monash . Join the pursuit of our purpose to build a better future for ourselves and our communities - #ChangeIt with us. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Mr Brodie Zambergs, Finance Applications & Processes Manager, +61 430 174 412 Position Description: Business Analysis and Improvements Officer Applications Close: Tuesday the 13th of October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698298/research-fellow": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Research Fellow Job No.: 698298 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 2-year fixed-term appointment with a potential extension of 1 year Remuneration: $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity An exciting opportunity exists for a Research Fellow to join the School of Physics and Astronomy at Monash University. This position is an exciting opportunity to join the team to work on the astrophysics of transients. This will include research related to searches for r-process element production in two different astrophysical sites: 1) kilonovae produced by neutron star mergers, and 2) collapsars produced by rapidly rotating massive stars that collapse and launch outflows rich in neutrons. The research fellow will be expected to connect to the broader activities of the Monash observational transient group, the national OzGrav Centre of Excellence transients working group, and international collaborators in supernova physics, stellar evolution, nucleosynthesis, and multi-messenger astronomy. To be appointable to this role, you must hold a doctoral qualification in astrophysics, physics, or a closely related field and have experience with transient astronomy, including analysis of observational data, comparison of multi-wavelength data against model predictions, and/or demonstrated knowledge of multi-messenger science. The successful candidate will also have demonstrated analytical and manuscript preparation skills and have a track record of refereed research publications, well-developed planning and organisational skills and excellent written and verbal communication skills. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Dr. Manisha Shrestha - Manisha.Shrestha@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow Applications Close: Tuesday 27th October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
