    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698870/masters-by-research-scholarship-in-engineering-science-ionic-liquids-for-safe-longlasting-sodium-metal-batteries": {
      "title": "Masters by Research Scholarship in Engineering Science - Ionic liquids for safe, long-lasting sodium metal batteries",
      "description": "Masters by Research Scholarship in Engineering Science - Ionic liquids for safe, long-lasting sodium metal batteries Job No.: 698870 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 2-year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: The successful applicant will receive a Research Living Allowance, at current value of $37,145AUD per annum 2026 full-time rate (tax-free stipend), indexed plus allowances as per RTP stipend scholarship conditions at: www.monash.edu/graduate-research/future-students/scholarships/scholarship-policy-and-procedures. The Opportunity Monash University is a leading global research and education institution known for turning ideas into real-world impact. It is a public research university and a member of Australia’s Group of Eight. Globally, it is ranked 31st. As the largest Australian university, Monash also has international campuses in Italy, China, India, Indonesia, and Malaysia. The Department of Materials Science and Engineering is ranked second in Australia, with broad research themes including metallurgy, biomaterials, polymer chemistry and processing, and advanced materials. Monash Engineering conducts world-class research in partnership with industry and leading institutions. Graduate researchers work on real-world challenges in a supportive environment with access to advanced facilities. Lithium-ion batteries currently dominate the energy storage market, but concerns regarding cost, safety, and critical mineral supply chains are driving the search for alternative technologies. Sodium metal batteries represent a promising next-generation solution. Sodium is abundant, low-cost, and sustainable and can offer high-energy densities comparable to current lithium-ion technologies. However, significant challenges remain in electrolyte design and interfacial stability, limiting commercial deployment. This project aims to develop advanced electrolyte systems based on safe and stable ionic liquids for high-energy sodium metal batteries capable of long-term operation in demanding environments. The outcomes will support the development of Australian battery technologies for applications including defence systems, agriculture, and off-grid energy storage. A fully funded Master's research opportunity (2 years) is available in the Faculty of Engineering at Monash University. The successful candidate will join a collaborative research environment under the supervision of Dr Mega Kar, working closely with researchers at Deakin University, international industry partner Solvionic (France), and Australian battery technology company DYNATI Energie. Candidate Requirements We are seeking a domestic Master's candidate with a desirable experience in: A strong interest in energy storage technologies including batteries Strong communication and problem-solving skills The ability to work both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary research team To apply for a domestic graduate research scholarship, you must be: an Australian citizen an Australian permanent resident a New Zealand citizen Applicants will be considered provided that they fulfil the criteria for Masters admission at Monash University in the Faculty of Engineering. Details of eligibility requirements can be found via https://www.monash.edu/engineering/future-students/graduate-research/how-to-apply Applicants should ensure they familiarise themselves with these requirements before deciding whether they should apply. The minimum qualifications for admission into a Master’s by Research course are: A bachelor’s degree of at least four years in a relevant discipline, which includes a research thesis or project; If English is not your first language, you will need to demonstrate English language proficiency; You will be required to show you have undertaken a research project or thesis as part of your academic education or have relevant professional experience Scholarship holders must be enrolled full-time and on campus. The successful applicant will be expected to enrol by March 2027. Enquiries Please direct enquiries to Dr Mega Kar, Senior Lecturer, Materials Science & Engineering, via mega.kar@monash.edu. Admission requirements and how to apply: Applications shall comprise: A cover letter that includes a brief statement of the applicant’s suitability A brief Research Proposal that fits within the broad project aims and objectives, and demonstrates some understanding of the area of research A curriculum vitae, including a list of any published works, conference presentations and relevant work experience A full statement of academic records, degree certificates and official grading system (if available), supported by scanned copies of relevant certified documentation English language proficiency test results (if applicable) Names and contact details of 2 academic referees Please submit your application via My.App and upload all supporting documentation in pdf format: Degree Information can be found via 3292 – Master of Engineering Science (Research) Select Admission Only as the Application Type Select ‘Yes’ to indicate that you are applying for a scholarship for this research and enter “Masters by Research Scholarship in Engineering Science - Ionic liquids for safe, long-lasting sodium metal batteries” as the scholarship name under Research Program Funding of the application Select Dr Mega Kar as the proposed main supervisor (final supervision arrangements to be confirmed upon appointment) Closing Date 30 November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT or earlier if the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed as they are received, and early applications are encouraged. #LI-DNI Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698830/dean-faculty-of-arts": {
      "title": "Dean, Faculty of Arts",
      "description": "Dean, Faculty of Arts Job No.: 698830 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term appointment Remuneration: A competitive remuneration package will apply Energise, connect and lead the Faculty into an exciting new era Be inspired and inspire others. Everyday Take your career in exciting, rewarding directions The Opportunity The next Dean will inherit a Faculty with exceptional strengths, distinctive momentum and extraordinary potential. The opportunity is to energise, connect and position the Faculty for an exciting new era of growth, influence and achievement. Reporting to the Provost and Senior Vice-President, the Dean will set the vision, direction and tone for the Faculty, with full accountability for performance, culture and outcomes. The Dean will be an influential contributor to Monash's senior leadership team, forging powerful partnerships across faculties, institutes and campuses, and ensuring the Faculty of the Arts is deeply connected to the broader University mission. Externally, the role will build momentum with government, industry, cultural institutions, philanthropy, alumni and community partners to unlock new possibilities, amplify impact and elevate the Faculty's profile nationally and internationally. Leading a complex and diverse academic community, the Dean will champion a culture of excellence, ambition and collaboration. They will be visible, accessible and deeply engaged with staff and students, supporting wellbeing and belonging while creating the conditions for bold ideas, strong performance and shared success. They will bring energy, optimism and clear direction, inspiring the Faculty to build on its considerable strengths and pursue its future with confidence. The Person We are seeking an accomplished and internationally recognised academic leader with a distinguished record of research excellence and a deep commitment to the humanities, arts and social sciences. The successful candidate will bring the strategic capability to shape and deliver a compelling vision, foster a high-performing and collaborative culture, and champion excellence across research, education and engagement. They will have a strong track record of building partnerships, supporting interdisciplinary initiatives, securing external opportunities and advocating effectively for staff, students and the broader Faculty community. Equally important will be their ability to navigate complexity, embrace innovation and emerging technologies, and position the Faculty for sustained impact, relevance and success in a rapidly changing global environment. This is an opportunity to lead a Faculty of significant scale, reputation and potential, and to help define the role of Arts in addressing the challenges and opportunities of the future. How to Apply For further information or a confidential discussion, please contact the consultants leading the search: Diana Kovalova on +61 421 643 624 or at diana.kovalova@odgers.com or Jenny Sutton at jenny.sutton@odgers.com Applications will close on Friday 16 October at 23.45 AEDT and should be submitted via the Odgers website www.odgers.com/96567 #LI-DNI Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/696991/senior-technical-officer-stm-genomics-and-bioinformatics": {
      "title": "Senior Technical Officer – STM Genomics and Bioinformatics",
      "description": "Senior Technical Officer – STM Genomics and Bioinformatics Job No.: 696991 Location: Alfred campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Continuing appointment Remuneration: $100,155 - $108,106 pa HEW Level 06 (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Join the School of Translational Medicine (STM) at the Alfred Campus as a Senior Technical Officer and help deliver high-quality genomics and bioinformatics services that enable researchers to turn complex data into meaningful scientific outcomes. Working across the STM Genomics and STM Bioinformatics Platforms , you’ll collaborate closely with the Genomics and Bioinformatics Managers to support research projects from sample and library quality assessment through to sequencing, data processing, analysis and delivery. What You'll Be Doing Support end-to-end NGS workflows, including sample reception, nucleic acid and library QC, library preparation, sequencing, primary data processing and data delivery. Operate, maintain and support NGS equipment, including Illumina sequencing platforms, and apply DNA and RNA library preparation methodologies such as WGS, RNA-seq, ATAC-seq and EM-seq. Support bioinformatics projects through data management, quality control, analysis, reporting and preparation of research outputs, including cost-recovered projects. Develop and maintain SOPs, workflows, technical documentation and sample management processes, while identifying and resolving technical issues and maintaining safe and compliant operations. Provide technical advice and support to researchers, staff and students, including assistance with bioinformatics training, workshops, teaching materials and researcher-facing resources. What You'll Bring A relevant degree in a scientific or technical field, or equivalent experience, with relevant expertise in genomics, molecular biology, bioinformatics or a related discipline. Demonstrated experience in DNA and RNA library construction for Illumina-based sequencing, with knowledge of methodologies including WGS, RNA-seq, ATAC-seq and EM-seq. Hands-on experience with nucleic acid and library quality control, including TapeStation, Qubit or equivalent instrumentation, and experience operating or supporting NGS instruments, particularly Illumina platforms. Strong computer literacy and experience with data management, bioinformatics tools and reproducible analysis practices for genomic or biomedical research datasets. Strong analytical, technical, investigative and problem-solving skills, with the ability to develop procedures, follow protocols and apply sound ethical and quality practices. Highly developed communication and organisational skills, with the ability to work independently and collaboratively, manage priorities and provide clear technical guidance and support to researchers and students. This is an opportunity to work at the intersection of genomics, bioinformatics and biomedical research , supporting projects that have the potential to make a real difference to human health. You’ll work alongside experienced specialists, build your expertise across complementary technical platforms, and contribute to innovative research while supporting the next generation of researchers through training and technical support. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Dr Justin Read, Manager, STM Genomics, E: justin.read@monash.edu Position Description: Senior Technical Officer Applications Close: Wednesday, 21st October at 11:55pm AEST Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698720/phd-stipend-scholarship-faculty-of-medicine-nursing-and-health-sciencesmonash-ai-institute-aienabled-personalised-coaching-for-physical-activity-and-other-health-behaviour-change-across-diverse-cultural-contexts": {
      "title": "PhD Stipend Scholarship – Faculty of Medicine, Nursing and Health Sciences/Monash AI institute [AI-enabled personalised coaching for physical activity and other health behaviour change across diverse cultural contexts]",
      "description": "PhD Stipend Scholarship – Faculty of Medicine, Nursing and Health Sciences/Monash AI institute [AI-enabled personalised coaching for physical activity and other health behaviour change across diverse cultural contexts] Job No.: 698720 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 3.5 years fixed-term appointment Supervisory Team: Lead Supervisor - Associate Professor Matthew Ahmadi; Co-supervisors: Dr Nicholas Koemel, Dr Syafiq Asnawi, Prof Emmanuel Stamatakis, Prof Adeeba Kamarulzaman, Dr Zhanna Sarsenbayeva, Assoc Prof Sam Liu, Prof Patrick Olivier Collaborating Supervisors & Team: Monash Institute of Medical Engineering, School of Psychological Sciences (The Turner Institute for Brain and Mental Health), School of Public Health and Preventive Medicine, Monash Faculty of Information Technology, and Monash University Malaysia Remuneration: Fully funded PhD Scholarship ($37,000 per annum). The stipend rate is indexed annually and published on the Monash University Graduate Research Stipend and Allowance Rates website. The Opportunity: We are recruiting PhD candidates to join our team at the School of Psychological Sciences working across AI-enabled personalised health. Supported by the Monash AI Institute, this program brings together researchers from Monash Medicine, Nursing and Health Sciences, Monash Information Technology, Monash University Malaysia, and industry partners to develop the next generation of personalised AI health coaching systems. Students will join a multidisciplinary PhD cohort spanning health, behavioural science, artificial intelligence, data science, and digital health, with opportunities to collaborate across disciplines, institutions, and industry. The PhD Project: Project Title: AI-Enabled Personalised Coaching for Physical Activity and other health Behaviour Change Across Diverse Cultural Contexts Project Description: This project explores the potential of AI-enabled personalised health coaching for building healthy sleep, physical activity, and dietary habits. The research will cover topics including: Human-AI interaction and trustworthy AI Intelligent wearables and multimodal sensing Culturally adaptive AI for behaviour change Translation into real-world community interventions This program includes work across medicine, computer science, and AI with an international team of researchers and industry partners to develop safe, scalable AI systems for behaviour change. Selection criteria: Prospective applicants must satisfy Monash University PhD entry requirements as outlined in the Monash Graduate Research Admission Procedure, including: Open to domestic candidates (Australian citizen, New Zealand citizen, or permanent resident status). A bachelor’s degree of at least four years in a relevant discipline, which includes a research thesis or project, with a minimum overall average grade of an Honours degree equivalent to the First Class Honours Division A; OR A master's degree in a relevant discipline which includes a research thesis or project equivalent to at least 25 percent of one year of full-time study, with a minimum overall average grade of honours equivalent to the First Class Honours Division A; OR A qualification, or combination of qualifications and relevant professional or research experience, deemed equivalent by the Graduate Research Committee (or delegate). How to apply We are seeking expressions of interest from qualified domestic candidates who wish to apply for this PhD opportunity. This position has a two-stage selection process: Stage 1: Submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) Candidates should contact the supervising researcher and include the following when submitting an EOI: A cover letter not exceeding 500 words stating your suitability and research interest. A curriculum vitae including peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, and relevant research/work experience. Scanned copies of academic transcripts. Contact details of two academic and/or clinical referees. Stage 2: Submit a PhD candidature application Candidates whose EOI is successful will be invited to submit a full application for Monash PhD candidature. Enquiries: Meghna Pathak ( Meghna.pathak@monash.edu ) Closing Date: Submit your interest before 31 October #LI-DNI Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698722/phd-stipend-scholarship-faculty-of-medicine-nursing-and-health-sciencesmonash-ai-institute-aienabled-personalised-coaching-for-physical-activity-sleep-and-health-behaviour-change": {
      "title": "PhD Stipend Scholarship – Faculty of Medicine, Nursing and Health Sciences/Monash AI institute [AI-enabled personalised coaching for physical activity, sleep and health behaviour change]",
      "description": "PhD Stipend Scholarship – Faculty of Medicine, Nursing and Health Sciences/Monash AI institute [AI-enabled personalised coaching for physical activity, sleep and health behaviour change] Job No.: 698722 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 3.5 years fixed-term appointment Supervisory Team: Lead Supervisor – Prof. Emmanuel Stamatakis; Co-supervisors: A/Prof. Matthew Ahmadi Collaborating Faculty/Team: Multidisciplinary team across the Faculty of Medicine, Nursing and Health Sciences, the Faculty of Information Technology, and Monash Malaysia Remuneration: Fully funded PhD Scholarship ($37,000 per annum). The stipend rate is indexed annually and published on the Monash University Graduate Research Stipend and Allowance Rates website. The Opportunity: This PhD stipend scholarship offers an outstanding opportunity for talented candidates to undertake interdisciplinary research within a cohort funded by the Monash AI Institute. The candidate will join a vibrant research team spanning medicine, health sciences, information technology, and behavioral science, with cross-institutional links to Monash Malaysia. The PhD Project: Project Title: AI-enabled personalised coaching for physical activity, sleep and health behaviour change Project Description: Digital behaviour change tools reach large populations cheaply, but most deliver generic advice and lose their users within weeks. Large language models and continuous wearable data now make genuinely personalised, context-aware coaching feasible for the first time, while raising real questions about safety, equity, engagement, and cultural fit. The candidate will co-design and evaluate an AI coaching system that uses wearable-derived physical activity and sleep data to generate personalised, just-in-time support, including scalable sleep interventions. Potential research activities include: Developing prediction models for receptivity and lapse Conducting micro-randomised or n-of-1 trials Evaluating psychological mechanisms of engagement and motivation Testing whether the system performs equitably across cultural and language groups Training spans intervention design, trial methodology, human-computer interaction, and applied machine learning. Selection criteria: Prospective applicants must satisfy Monash University PhD entry requirements as outlined in the Monash Graduate Research Admission Procedure, including: Open to domestic candidates (Australian citizen, New Zealand citizen, or permanent resident status). A bachelor’s degree of at least four years in a relevant discipline, which includes a research thesis or project, with a minimum overall average grade of an Honours degree equivalent to the First Class Honours Division A; OR A master's degree in a relevant discipline which includes a research thesis or project equivalent to at least 25 percent of one year of full-time study, with a minimum overall average grade of honours equivalent to the First Class Honours Division A; OR A qualification, or combination of qualifications and relevant professional or research experience, deemed equivalent by the Graduate Research Committee (or delegate). Background or experience in digital health, behavioral science, physical activity/sleep research, machine learning, or human-computer interaction would be advantageous. How to apply We are seeking expressions of interest from qualified domestic candidates who wish to apply for this PhD opportunity. This position has a two-stage selection process: Stage 1: Submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) Candidates should contact the supervising researcher and include the following when submitting an EOI: A cover letter not exceeding 500 words stating your suitability and research interest. A curriculum vitae including peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, and relevant research/work experience. Scanned copies of academic transcripts. Contact details of two academic and/or clinical referees. Stage 2: Submit a PhD candidature application Candidates whose EOI is successful will be invited to submit a full application for Monash PhD candidature. Enquiries: Meghna Pathak ( Meghna.pathak@monash.edu ) Closing Date: Submit your interest before 31 October #LI-DNI Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698734/executive-director-financial-control-and-operations": {
      "title": "EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, FINANCIAL CONTROL AND OPERATIONS",
      "description": "EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, FINANCIAL CONTROL AND OPERATIONS Job No.: 398734 Location: Mulgrave Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term appointment Remuneration: A competitive remuneration package will apply Australia's largest and most international university Lead global financial control, operations and transformation Underpin long-term financial sustainability and resilience About the role Reporting to the Chief Financial Officer and Senior Vice-President, the Executive Director, Financial Control and Operations is a key member of the Finance Leadership Team, responsible for financial control, reporting, tax, finance systems and finance operations across the Monash Group. Leading a team of approximately 90 through four direct reports, the role oversees statutory and external reporting, group accounting, audit and regulatory compliance across multiple jurisdictions, while providing trusted advice to the Executive team, Finance Committee and Risk and Audit Committee. The Executive Director will also lead significant finance transformation initiatives, including the finance stream of a major ERP implementation, advancing automation and digital capability, strengthening controls and governance, and modernising finance operations. A strategic focus on international tax, leadership capability and continuous improvement will be central to ensuring finance supports Monash's long-term sustainability and global ambitions. About you CA or CPA qualified you are a senior finance executive with extensive experience leading broad finance functions in large, complex and multi-jurisdictional organisations. You combine deep technical accounting expertise with a strong track record in finance operations, transformation and organisational change. A Big 4 foundation, complemented by substantial industry experience, is expected. You bring sophisticated knowledge of financial reporting, governance and international operations, together with experience leading enterprise-scale technology and ERP-enabled transformation. A proven leader of leaders, you build high-performing teams, develop capability and influence effectively across complex stakeholder environments. Combining sound judgement, commercial acumen and intellectual curiosity, you communicate complex financial matters with clarity and confidence, enabling informed decision-making at executive, committee and board level. How to apply Monash University is partnering with, and has exclusively appointed, SHK to lead the search for this key appointment. Click on the link below to submit your CV along with a covering letter. For more information prior to application, please contact Andrea Ogden at SHK via email on andrea.ogden@shk.com.au , quoting reference #3669176 . Direct and third-party applications will be forwarded to SHK. Executive Director, Financial Control and Operations job in Accounting & Finance in Melbourne Applications close AEST 11.59pm on Sunday 18 th October 2026 #LI-DNI Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698677/phd-scholarship-in-the-study-of-islamophobia-in-australia": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship in the Study of Islamophobia in Australia",
      "description": "PhD Scholarship in the Study of Islamophobia in Australia Job No: 698677 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 3.5 years fixed-term appointment Remuneration: The successful applicant will receive a Research Living Allowance, at current value of $37,145AUD per annum 2026 full-time rate (tax-free stipend), indexed plus allowances as per RTP stipend scholarship conditions at: https://www.monash.edu/graduate-research/future-students/scholarships/scholarship-policy-and-procedures . A tuition fee scholarship and Single Overseas Health Cover (OSHC) will be provided for a successful international awardee. The Opportunity This PhD scholarship is funded as an important part of an ARC funded project Tracking Islamophobia and its Impacts on Australian Muslims’ Lives (LP250100022), conducted by Dr Susan Carland, Professor Kerry O’Brien, Dr Zarina Vakhitova, and Associate Professor Matteo Vergani. The project will establish Australia’s first national longitudinal cohort study of the drivers and consequences of Islamophobia. It will examine environmental drivers (e.g., social and traditional media messages, international events) of Islamophobia over time, and how experiences of Islamophobia affect the lives and outcomes for Australian Muslims. The project will be conducted with Muslim community and industry partners, and government. The PhD candidate will have the opportunity to work on different aspects of the project and will receive excellent training as well as opportunities to be co-author on resulting publications (minimum 3) from their PhD studies. Undertaking this PhD as part of a larger project has several advantages. First, the successful PhD candidate will be integrated into an established research program funded by the Australian Research Council, with access to a large longitudinal dataset and to funding for qualitative fieldwork, publications, and conference attendance. Second, the PhD candidate will benefit from expert supervision from research leaders in sociology, psychology, criminology and computational social sciences. Third, there is the potential for the candidate to undertake an industry placement with one of the project’s partner organisations, and to gain applied research and industry experience alongside university research training. Finally, the candidate will benefit from publication mentoring co-authorship on publications related to the PhD project. They will also be able to participate in funded symposia, community engagement events, and potential future grant applications. Monash University is the largest university in Australia and consistently ranks in the top 100 universities worldwide. Monash has six globally networked campuses and international alliances in Europe and Asia. The applicant will be based at the Clayton campus in Melbourne. Social Science at Monash is diverse and vibrant, including research expertise in geography, sociology, politics and sustainability transitions. We have a strong and supportive research culture, led by internationally recognised scholars successful in attracting national and international competitive funding. Candidate Requirements The successful applicant will have an excellent academic track record in a relevant Social Sciences discipline (e.g., empirical Sociology, Psychology, Criminology or other related cognate disciplines). Demonstrable experience in conducting research with, or working alongside, Australian Muslim communities, and strong familiarity with the experiences of those communities. Proficiency with quantitative and/or qualitative research methodologies is required. Highly competitive applicants will hold an Australian (or equivalent international) Honour’s or Master’s (with research thesis) degree (both in a relevant field), with a significant research component and with first-class honours/H1 awarded. Details of eligibility requirements, including English-language proficiency skills, to undertake a PhD in the Faculty of Arts are available at https://arts.monash.edu/graduate-research/application-process/ . Applicants should ensure they familiarise themselves with these requirements before deciding whether they should apply. Scholarship holders must be enrolled full-time and on campus. Please note: applicants who already hold a PhD will not be considered. The successful applicant will be expected to enrol by 2 February 2027 . There may be some flexibility as to the date of commencement. Enquiries Dr Susan Carland, School of Social Sciences, Email: susan.carland@monash.edu How to Apply Applications shall comprise: A cover letter that includes a brief statement of the applicant’s suitability A brief Research Proposal that fits within the broad project aims and objectives, and demonstrates some understanding of the area of research A curriculum vitae, including a list of any published works, conference presentations and relevant work experience A full statement of academic records, degree certificates and official grading system (if available), supported by scanned copies of relevant certified documentation English language proficiency test results (if applicable) Names and contact details of 2 academic referees Please submit your application via My.App and upload all supporting documentation in pdf format : Select Admission Only as the Application Type Select ‘Yes’ to indicate that you are applying for a scholarship for this research and enter “Tracking Islamophobia PhD Scholarship” as the scholarship name under Research Program Funding of the application. Select Dr Susan Carland as the proposed main supervisor (final supervision arrangements to be confirmed upon appointment). Applications Close: Sunday 1st November 2026, 11:55 pm AEST Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698514/phd-selfevolving-agents-continual-trustworthy-and-resourceefficient-agentic-ai": {
      "title": "PhD: Self-Evolving Agents: Continual, Trustworthy, and Resource-Efficient Agentic AI",
      "description": "PhD: Self-Evolving Agents: Continual, Trustworthy, and Resource-Efficient Agentic AI Job No.: 698514 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: The scholarship may be held for up to 4 years (fulltime) for Research Doctorate (PhD) studies. Remuneration: The successful applicant will receive: A four year scholarship package totalling approximately $48,000 AUD per annum (2026 rate with annual indexation) A four year Project Expense and Development package starting from $13,000 per annum. FIT Candidature Funding of $4,000 AUD for the duration of the candidature Up to $1,265 AUD from Monash Graduate Research Office as a one-off travel grant The Opportunity As part of the CSIRO Industry PhD scholarship program , this is an unprecedented opportunity for an outstanding data science and AI PhD candidate interested in agentic AI, supervised by Dr Teresa Wang and Dr Tongtong Wu at Monash University, jointly with Dr He Zhao and Dr, Dan Steinberg from CSIRO, Dr Yue Yang and David Lemphers from Maincode. Advances in large language models and agentic AI have enabled autonomous systems that can reason, plan, retrieve information, and use external tools. However, most existing agent frameworks remain largely static: their knowledge, internal organisation, and coordination strategies are typically predefined. This makes them brittle in dynamic environments where information, tasks, and requirements continuously evolve. This project investigates self-evolving agentic AI systems, i.e., adaptive AI agents that can continually acquire new knowledge, reorganise their collaboration structures, and improve their performance over time. The student will be able work on cutting-edge AI/ML/LLM topics closely with leading experts in Monash University, CSIRO, and Maincode and access to the computational resources in these organisations. The student will also gain valuable industrial experience from Maincode who could provide a solid testbed for the work, which would help transition the research into real-world applications. To be considered for this opportunity you should fulfil the eligibility requirements listed below. Be an Australian citizen or Permanent Resident, or a New Zealand citizen. A bachelor’s degree of at least four years in a relevant discipline, which includes a research thesis or project, with a minimum overall average grade of an honours degree equivalent to the First Class Honours; or A master's degree in a relevant discipline which includes a research thesis or project equivalent to at least 25 percent of one year of full-time study, with a minimum overall average grade of honours equivalent to the First Class Honours; or A qualification, or combination of qualifications and relevant professional experience, deemed equivalent by the GRC (or delegate). Meet university English language requirements. Not have previously completed a PhD. Be able to commence the Program in the year of the offer. Enrol as a full-time PhD student. Part-time arrangements may be considered if approved by the supervisory team and in accordance with university policy. Be prepared to be located at the project location(s) that Monash University has approved and, if required, comply with Monash’s external enrolment procedures. Be prepared to undergo onboarding to CSIRO, which will include passing mandatory government background checks (allow for between 4 to 8 weeks) and complete any other CSIRO requirements. The ideal PhD candidate will have: A strong background in computer science, artificial intelligence, machine learning, or a closely related field. A solid understanding of machine learning concepts, particularly deep learning and large language models, is desirable. Experience with Python and common machine learning frameworks (such as PyTorch or TensorFlow) is highly valued. Familiarity with topics such as natural language processing, multi-agent systems, reinforcement learning, or knowledge representation would be advantageous but is not strictly required. Strong analytical and problem-solving abilities, as well as an interest in developing and evaluating new AI methods. Experience with research projects, publications, or advanced coursework in AI or data science will be considered favourably. Good programming skills and strong written and verbal communication skills. Enthusiasm for interdisciplinary research and the ability to collaborate effectively within a research team are also important Monash University strongly advocates diversity, equality, fairness and openness . We fully support the gender equity principles of the Athena SWAN Charter . The Project This project investigates self-evolving agentic AI systems, i.e., adaptive AI agents that can continually acquire new knowledge, reorganise their collaboration structures, and improve their performance over time. The research will explore methods for continual knowledge integration, adaptive multi-agent orchestration, and knowledge-grounded reasoning using structured resources such as ontologies and knowledge graphs. It will also study approaches for building efficient and trustworthy agent systems that can scale to complex tasks. The project aims to develop a unified framework for building agentic AI systems that can operate as long-term collaborators, continuously learning and adapting as their environments and objectives change. By combining ideas from continual learning, multi-agent systems, and knowledge-grounded reasoning, this research seeks to advance the foundations of adaptive AI systems capable of sustained, reliable performance in evolving knowledge environments. This position has a two-stage selection process: Stage 1: Please submit to fit-graduate.research@monash.edu . With the EOI please include the documents - CV, academic transcripts and grading scale, a cover letter and a draft research proposal of up to 2 pages, responding to one or more of the above research objectives. The draft research proposal should outline your interest in being a PhD candidate and summarise the theoretical and methodological approaches you are interested in pursuing, such as continual learning, multi-agent systems, and knowledge-grounded reasoning. Stage 2: Candidates who pass this stage of the selection process will be invited to discuss their ideas with the supervisory team before developing and submitting a full application. Enquiries: fit-graduate.research@monash.edu or supervisor email address Further information: Visit CSIRO iPhD website Or iPhD project page Applications Close: Friday 13th November 2026, 11:55 pm AEST Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/686949/lecturer-education-focused-monash-rural-health-churchill": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Education Focused), Monash Rural Health Churchill",
      "description": "Lecturer (Education Focused), Monash Rural Health Churchill Job No.: 686949 Location: Monash Rural Health Churchill Employment Type: Part-time, fraction (0.7) Duration: Continuing appointment Remuneration: Pro-rata of $123,138 - $146,228 pa Level B (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact as an Education-focused Lecturer Join our inclusive, collaborative medical education community Shape the future of medicine education through exceptional teaching The Opportunity The School of Rural Health is seeking an Education-Focused Lecturer to contribute to the delivery and ongoing development of medicine education at Monash Rural Health in Churchill. This position will play an important role in medicine course assessment and contribute to teaching the pharmacology component of the Year A program, supporting a high-quality student learning experience within a contemporary medical education environment. The position will contribute to the design and implementation of high-quality assessment and evaluation processes that support effective learning and provide meaningful insights into the student experience. Working collaboratively across the Years 1 and 2 programs, the position will liaise with colleagues at Clayton, Mildura and in Malaysia to support consistency and alignment in content delivery and assessment, while contributing to evidence-informed approaches to educational design and delivery. The position is suited to an education-focused academic with a relevant tertiary qualification and demonstrated knowledge and skills in contemporary teaching, assessment and evaluation practices within a higher education or medical education setting. A sound understanding of pharmacology and the ability to work collaboratively across academic teams will support the successful delivery of the role. Join Monash Rural Health and contribute to the delivery of high-quality medical education and assessment that supports the learning and development of future health professionals. We look forward to receiving your application. Travelling to various University campuses may be part of the position, and possessing a valid driver's license is crucial for performing the responsibilities. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Associate Professor Marianne Tare, Director - Monash Rural Health Churchill, +61 3 5122 7485 Position Description: Lecturer (Education Focused) Lecturer (Education Focused), Monash Rural Health Churchill Job No.: 686949 Location: Monash Rural Health Churchill Employment Type: Part-time, fraction (0.7) Duration: Continuing appointment Remuneration: Pro-rata of $123,138 - $146,228 pa Level B (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact as an Education-focused Lecturer Join our inclusive, collaborative medical education community Shape the future of medicine education through exceptional teaching The Opportunity The School of Rural Health is seeking an Education-Focused Lecturer to contribute to the delivery and ongoing development of medicine education at Monash Rural Health in Churchill. This position will play an important role in medicine course assessment and contribute to teaching the pharmacology component of the Year A program, supporting a high-quality student learning experience within a contemporary medical education environment. The position will contribute to the design and implementation of high-quality assessment and evaluation processes that support effective learning and provide meaningful insights into the student experience. Working collaboratively across the Years 1 and 2 programs, the position will liaise with colleagues at Clayton, Mildura and in Malaysia to support consistency and alignment in content delivery and assessment, while contributing to evidence-informed approaches to educational design and delivery. The position is suited to an education-focused academic with a relevant tertiary qualification and demonstrated knowledge and skills in contemporary teaching, assessment and evaluation practices within a higher education or medical education setting. A sound understanding of pharmacology and the ability to work collaboratively across academic teams will support the successful delivery of the role. Join Monash Rural Health and contribute to the delivery of high-quality medical education and assessment that supports the learning and development of future health professionals. We look forward to receiving your application. Travelling to various University campuses may be part of the position, and possessing a valid driver's license is crucial for performing the responsibilities. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Associate Professor Marianne Tare, Director - Monash Rural Health Churchill, +61 3 5122 7485 Position Description: Lecturer (Education Focused) Applications Close: Thursday 15 October 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
